From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 01/21] dndcp: Validate file lists and file names from the host
 and drag sources

These helpers take their input from the host RPC channel without
checking it, and the X11 and GTK3 backends share them. A later patch in
this series turns on the capability bits that make
LocalPrepareFileContents() reachable from a Wayland session.

DnDFileList::FromCPClipboard() casts its buffer to a CPFileList and uses
relPathsLen and fulPathsLen, both uint32 values from the same buffer, as
lengths to copy. Neither is checked against the number of bytes
received, and the header is read before the buffer is known to hold one.
GetRelPathsStr() and GetFullPathsStr() then return the bytes read past
the end. Check the header length first, then each field against what
remains, with the second test ordered so that the subtraction cannot
underflow. AttributesFromCPClipboard() has the same shape and gets the
same check.

LocalPrepareFileContents() joins a host supplied cpName onto the staging
directory and then creates, writes and chmods the result. Nothing
rejects an absolute path or a .. component, so a name can point the
write outside the staging directory. cpName is opaque<> in the XDR
definition and so has no terminator, yet it is used as a C string. The
new LocalIsSafeStagingName() rejects a name that is unterminated, empty,
absolute, or has a .. component. The name is also published in a
text/uri-list, where CR and LF each end an entry, so it rejects those
too.

The same check now runs wherever a host file list is published as paths
below a staging directory. That is the GTK4 host to guest drag, the GTK3
one, and a host file paste in the X11 copy and paste backend for GTK3
and GTK4. A list with an unusable name is refused as a whole. The X11
copy and paste backends also check the result of FromCPClipboard(),
which they ignored.

In the GTK4 drag, OnSrcDragBegin() returned without telling the host
when it could not use the list, which left the drag state machine in
DRAGBEGIN_PENDING until the host's next drag reset it. It now cancels
the drag with the host and resets the state machine, for the new check
and for the two earlier returns.

Four files carry the same RFC 1630 escape table, and none of them
escapes CR or LF, which dnd/dnd.h defines as the record separators of
that format in DND_URI_LIST_POST. The four are dndUIX11.cpp,
dndUIX11GTK4.cpp, copyPasteUIX11.cpp and copyPasteUIX11GTK4.cpp, which
cover both directions of drag and drop and of copy and paste, for GTK3
and GTK4. Fix all four, since configure decides which of them is built.
A name containing CR or LF is published as several entries, and the
extra entries can name any absolute path, outside the staging directory
and outside the block. Escape both characters.

The lists that come from guest applications need checks too. A dragged
or copied URI with no path separator, such as "file:foo", made
Str_Strrchr() return NULL, and the parsers used NULL + 1 as the relative
path. All four now skip such an entry. SetCPClipboardFromGtk_File() also
read totalSize uninitialised. Trimming the GNOME list separator from an
empty host file list made erase() throw out of the main loop, and the
trim now checks that the separator is there.

Malformed input makes these functions return failure rather than assert.
They run on the daemon's main loop, and an assertion failure on one bad
message would stop the daemon.

Only the GTK4 configuration was compiled. dndUIX11.cpp and
copyPasteUIX11.cpp are built only for GTK3 and were not.
